From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 02:04:44 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: copy a user string without padding the rest of its page

strncpy_chunk_from_user() copied each chunk with strncpy(), which writes
zeros after the terminator up to the length it is given, and that length
runs to the end of the user page. getname() asks for EMBEDDED_NAME_MAX
bytes, so every system call that takes a path wrote up to 4064 bytes
beyond the name, however short the name was: with 16 KiB pages three of
every four start offsets pay for all 4064 bytes, and with 4 KiB pages
the fill averages about half of that. lib/string.c's strncpy() keeps
rereading the terminator through a select on the byte it last loaded, so
each byte of the fill waits for the load before it. The fill was 80% to
87% of the kernel time of stat() on an A15 and 86% on an M1, at 0.6 to
1.7 ns a byte on the A15, varying from one boot to the next, and 2.2 ns
on the M1: 2.4 to 7 microseconds a call on the one and 9 on the other.

Measure the string, copy only its bytes and terminate it. The native
do_strncpy_from_user() does not pad either: it zeroes at most the rest
of the word that holds the terminator, and neither version copies a
source byte past it. strnlen() and memcpy() are the host's, which
strnlen_chunk() and the copy chunks beside it already use on the same
physical alias, and neither reads outside the chunk.

A path now costs the same wherever it sits in its page. On the A15 with
the gadget engine, a stat() loop takes 24% less time, ls -laR /usr 19%,
PATH lookups in dash 28%, tar 16% and find 6%; Python imports and
compute are unchanged. These times were measured on a kernel without
frame pointers; the change does not depend on them.
---
 arch/um/kernel/skas/uaccess.c | 15 +++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/kernel/skas/uaccess.c b/arch/um/kernel/skas/uaccess.c
index 56db2f7003bc8aa93c7009c9b021aaf0d4e5074f..1c2a4d1fe496894487f6f0a34874a823d006bf9d 100644
--- a/arch/um/kernel/skas/uaccess.c
+++ b/arch/um/kernel/skas/uaccess.c
@@ -444,12 +444,19 @@ static int strncpy_chunk_from_user(unsigned long from, int len, void *arg)
 	char **to_ptr = arg, *to = *to_ptr;
 	int n;
 
-	strncpy(to, (void *) from, len);
-	n = strnlen(to, len);
+	/*
+	 * Copy only the string.  strncpy() would also zero the rest of the
+	 * chunk, which runs to the end of the user page, one byte at a time.
+	 * The native strncpy_from_user() does not pad either.
+	 */
+	n = strnlen((void *) from, len);
+	memcpy(to, (void *) from, n);
 	*to_ptr += n;
 
-	if (n < len)
-	        return 1;
+	if (n < len) {
+		to[n] = '\0';
+		return 1;
+	}
 	return 0;
 }
 
